1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,734
Hello again,
hackers.
Before we name the

2
00:00:02,734 --> 00:00:05,126
bug,
picture a program's memory as a

3
00:00:05,126 --> 00:00:07,603
wall of numbered spaces.
A value can

4
00:00:07,603 --> 00:00:10,166
live in one space.
The number written

5
00:00:10,166 --> 00:00:12,729
on its door is an address.
Everything

6
00:00:12,729 --> 00:00:15,292
else in this story follows from that.

7
00:00:15,292 --> 00:00:17,286
An address is not the value.
It

8
00:00:17,286 --> 00:00:19,612
is a direction:
go to this location.

9
00:00:19,612 --> 00:00:22,105
A pointer is just a variable holding

10
00:00:22,105 --> 00:00:24,846
that direction.
Copy the pointer,
and you

11
00:00:24,846 --> 00:00:28,003
have copied the direction,
not duplicated the

12
00:00:28,003 --> 00:00:29,167
thing it reaches.

13
00:00:29,167 --> 00:00:31,660
On the heap,
programs ask for a

14
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:34,777
fresh patch of storage,
then build an

15
00:00:34,777 --> 00:00:38,517
object inside it.
This challenge calls that

16
00:00:38,517 --> 00:00:42,257
object user.
It contains two directions:
one

17
00:00:42,257 --> 00:00:44,334
to a function,
and one to a

18
00:00:44,334 --> 00:00:44,750
name.

19
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:47,040
Some objects have a lifetime:
they are

20
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:49,995
created,
used,
and eventually released.
Free says

21
00:00:49,995 --> 00:00:52,654
the program has finished with that storage.

22
00:00:52,654 --> 00:00:55,166
It does not magically erase every copied

23
00:00:55,166 --> 00:00:58,416
pointer elsewhere.
Those directions can outlive the

24
00:00:58,416 --> 00:01:00,042
object they meant to reach.

25
00:01:00,042 --> 00:01:02,140
Now we earn the real challenge.
The

26
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:04,689
local source defines cmd with a function

27
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:07,612
pointer named whatToDo followed by a username

28
00:01:07,612 --> 00:01:10,686
pointer.
The program's menu chooses whatToDo,
then

29
00:01:10,686 --> 00:01:13,984
doProcess calls whichever function address sits in

30
00:01:13,984 --> 00:01:14,958
the first field.

31
00:01:14,958 --> 00:01:17,950
That indirect call is ordinary behavior.
Pick

32
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:20,391
Subscribe,
and the first field holds s.

33
00:01:20,391 --> 00:01:22,989
doProcess reads that field,
then calls s.

34
00:01:22,989 --> 00:01:25,587
The surprise is not the instruction.
The

35
00:01:25,587 --> 00:01:28,185
surprise is allowing the same memory to

36
00:01:28,185 --> 00:01:29,917
later mean something else.

37
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:33,348
Delete is the lifetime mistake.
It runs

38
00:01:33,348 --> 00:01:35,708
free on user,
but user is not

39
00:01:35,708 --> 00:01:38,818
cleared.
The main loop keeps the old

40
00:01:38,818 --> 00:01:43,108
direction.
The program has released the storage,

41
00:01:43,108 --> 00:01:46,861
yet still believes that old address names

42
00:01:46,861 --> 00:01:48,792
a live command object.

43
00:01:48,792 --> 00:01:51,272
Later,
Leave a message asks for eight

44
00:01:51,272 --> 00:01:53,999
bytes.
The allocator is allowed to reuse

45
00:01:53,999 --> 00:01:56,975
compatible freed storage.
If it returns the

46
00:01:56,975 --> 00:01:59,290
old user space,
the message and the

47
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:02,265
stale user pointer describe the same bytes

48
00:02:02,265 --> 00:02:04,167
from two different stories.

49
00:02:04,167 --> 00:02:06,820
Writing the message changes bytes the old

50
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:09,169
pointer reads as a command.
Four bytes

51
00:02:09,169 --> 00:02:12,125
become the function destination.
The next four

52
00:02:12,125 --> 00:02:14,778
fill the second field.
When doProcess runs

53
00:02:14,778 --> 00:02:17,128
again,
stale data sends control to the

54
00:02:17,128 --> 00:02:18,417
challenge function.

55
00:02:18,417 --> 00:02:21,710
That is use-after-free:
use happens after the

56
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:25,645
object's lifetime ends.
The repair is architectural:

57
00:02:25,645 --> 00:02:28,664
retire every owner after free,
use clear

58
00:02:28,664 --> 00:02:32,415
lifetime rules,
and validate before indirect use.

59
00:02:32,415 --> 00:02:35,709
Make addresses and ownership tell the same

60
00:02:35,709 --> 00:02:36,167
story.

